const _astro_dataLayerContent = [["Map",1,2,9,10],"meta::meta",["Map",3,4,5,6,7,8],"astro-version","5.13.7","content-config-digest","51efe376fecfa1f2","astro-config-digest","{\"root\":{},\"srcDir\":{},\"publicDir\":{},\"outDir\":{},\"cacheDir\":{},\"site\":\"https://joannahosking.com\",\"compressHTML\":true,\"base\":\"/\",\"trailingSlash\":\"ignore\",\"output\":\"static\",\"scopedStyleStrategy\":\"attribute\",\"build\":{\"format\":\"directory\",\"client\":{},\"server\":{},\"assets\":\"_astro\",\"serverEntry\":\"entry.mjs\",\"redirects\":true,\"inlineStylesheets\":\"auto\",\"concurrency\":1},\"server\":{\"open\":false,\"host\":false,\"port\":4321,\"streaming\":true,\"allowedHosts\":[]},\"redirects\":{},\"image\":{\"endpoint\":{\"route\":\"/_image\"},\"service\":{\"entrypoint\":\"astro/assets/services/sharp\",\"config\":{}},\"domains\":[],\"remotePatterns\":[],\"responsiveStyles\":false},\"devToolbar\":{\"enabled\":true},\"markdown\":{\"syntaxHighlight\":{\"type\":\"shiki\",\"excludeLangs\":[\"math\"]},\"shikiConfig\":{\"langs\":[],\"langAlias\":{},\"theme\":\"github-dark\",\"themes\":{},\"wrap\":false,\"transformers\":[]},\"remarkPlugins\":[],\"rehypePlugins\":[],\"remarkRehype\":{},\"gfm\":true,\"smartypants\":true},\"security\":{\"checkOrigin\":true},\"env\":{\"schema\":{},\"validateSecrets\":false},\"experimental\":{\"clientPrerender\":false,\"contentIntellisense\":false,\"headingIdCompat\":false,\"preserveScriptOrder\":false,\"liveContentCollections\":false,\"csp\":false,\"staticImportMetaEnv\":false,\"chromeDevtoolsWorkspace\":false},\"legacy\":{\"collections\":false}}","projects",["Map",11,12,36,37,60,61,82,83,105,106],"palette",{id:11,data:13,body:19,filePath:20,assetImports:21,digest:26,rendered:27,legacyId:35},{title:14,shortname:15,description:16,icon:17,sortOrder:18},"Hue-shifted palette generator","Palette generator","hue-shifted palette generator","__ASTRO_IMAGE_design",2,"- [View on Github &rarr;](https://github.com/joannahosking/palette-generator)\r\n- [Visit demo &rarr;](https://palette.joannahosking.com/)\r\n\r\nAs I enjoy pixel art as a hobby, this tool was built to assist with making palette choices that would enhance depth and contrast in my projects. While there is much more to [colour theory](https://pixelparmesan.com/color-theory-for-pixel-artists-its-all-relative/), I've found hue shifting to be a good starting point for a novice in building palettes. The general idea is that highlighted areas have warmer values, and shadows have cooler values.\r\n\r\n![Screenshot](../../assets/projectImages/palette-generator.png)\r\n\r\nUsers are able to control the primary colour and amount of shift, which is dynamically generated by a series of helper functions for both the primary colour and two additional palettes. As the primary colour and shift are adjusted, hex values are generated for the user to copy to their clipboard.\r\n\r\n![Shift amount adjustment](../../assets/projectImages/palette-hue-shifting.gif)\r\n*Shift value adjustment results in more or less monochromatic palettes.*\r\n\r\nThe project utilises the _chroma-js_ package to calculate the LCH value of the selected colour, then each shade is generated by shifting the hue by the selected step and incrementing the lightness equally. A complementary and triadic palette is also created by adjusting the hue of the primary colour.\r\n\r\n![Primary colour adjustment](../../assets/projectImages/palette-changing-primary.gif)\r\n*The primary colour is adjusted with an HTML colour input.*\r\n\r\nVisually, the colour picker and 'copy to clipboard' icons became an accessibility challenge as a dark or light icon wouldn't meet readability standards toward their respective lightness. Thanks to LCH measuring relative lightness, the swatch component is able to simply measure the lightness value  of each shade and choose an icon fill colour appropriately. This can be seen in the screenshot above.\r\n\r\nI recently rebuilt the project in Next.js as an exercise. The most useful addition since has been exporting a generated palette straight to Procreate. Procreate reads palettes from a `.swatches` file, which is really just a JSON structure zipped up with a specific internal layout, so the export button builds that structure from the current palette state in the browser and triggers a download, no server involved. It's saved me a fair amount of manual swatch-building time mid-project, which was the actual motivation for adding it.","src/content/projects/palette.md",[22,23,24,25],"../../assets/projectImages/palette-generator.png","../../assets/projectImages/palette-hue-shifting.gif","../../assets/projectImages/palette-changing-primary.gif","design","e945c1a8be533883",{html:28,metadata:29},"<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/joannahosking/palette-generator\">View on Github →</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://palette.joannahosking.com/\">Visit demo →</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>As I enjoy pixel art as a hobby, this tool was built to assist with making palette choices that would enhance depth and contrast in my projects. While there is much more to <a href=\"https://pixelparmesan.com/color-theory-for-pixel-artists-its-all-relative/\">colour theory</a>, I’ve found hue shifting to be a good starting point for a novice in building palettes. The general idea is that highlighted areas have warmer values, and shadows have cooler values.</p>\n<p><img __ASTRO_IMAGE_=\"{&#x22;src&#x22;:&#x22;../../assets/projectImages/palette-generator.png&#x22;,&#x22;alt&#x22;:&#x22;Screenshot&#x22;,&#x22;index&#x22;:0}\"></p>\n<p>Users are able to control the primary colour and amount of shift, which is dynamically generated by a series of helper functions for both the primary colour and two additional palettes. As the primary colour and shift are adjusted, hex values are generated for the user to copy to their clipboard.</p>\n<p><img __ASTRO_IMAGE_=\"{&#x22;src&#x22;:&#x22;../../assets/projectImages/palette-hue-shifting.gif&#x22;,&#x22;alt&#x22;:&#x22;Shift amount adjustment&#x22;,&#x22;index&#x22;:0}\">\r\n<em>Shift value adjustment results in more or less monochromatic palettes.</em></p>\n<p>The project utilises the <em>chroma-js</em> package to calculate the LCH value of the selected colour, then each shade is generated by shifting the hue by the selected step and incrementing the lightness equally. A complementary and triadic palette is also created by adjusting the hue of the primary colour.</p>\n<p><img __ASTRO_IMAGE_=\"{&#x22;src&#x22;:&#x22;../../assets/projectImages/palette-changing-primary.gif&#x22;,&#x22;alt&#x22;:&#x22;Primary colour adjustment&#x22;,&#x22;index&#x22;:0}\">\r\n<em>The primary colour is adjusted with an HTML colour input.</em></p>\n<p>Visually, the colour picker and ‘copy to clipboard’ icons became an accessibility challenge as a dark or light icon wouldn’t meet readability standards toward their respective lightness. Thanks to LCH measuring relative lightness, the swatch component is able to simply measure the lightness value  of each shade and choose an icon fill colour appropriately. This can be seen in the screenshot above.</p>\n<p>I recently rebuilt the project in Next.js as an exercise. The most useful addition since has been exporting a generated palette straight to Procreate. Procreate reads palettes from a <code>.swatches</code> file, which is really just a JSON structure zipped up with a specific internal layout, so the export button builds that structure from the current palette state in the browser and triggers a download, no server involved. It’s saved me a fair amount of manual swatch-building time mid-project, which was the actual motivation for adding it.</p>",{headings:30,localImagePaths:31,remoteImagePaths:32,frontmatter:33,imagePaths:34},[],[22,23,24],[],{title:14,shortname:15,description:16,icon:25,sortOrder:18},[22,23,24],"palette.md","santa",{id:36,data:38,body:44,filePath:45,assetImports:46,digest:50,rendered:51,legacyId:59},{title:39,shortname:40,description:41,icon:42,sortOrder:43},"Santa dash pixel christmas card","Santa dash","pixel game christmas card","__ASTRO_IMAGE_game",5,"- [Play now &rarr;](https://christmas.cooperfields.co.uk/)\r\n\r\nEvery year, we put out a Christmas card for our clients at work. It's usually a message wishing them a happy holiday season, but for 2023 we decided to do something a bit more creative.\r\n\r\n![Christmas Card](../../assets/projectImages/christmas-card.png)\r\n*Animated Christmas card cover*\r\n\r\nSanta Dash is a simple pixel game built in canvas where the user collects coins and gifts to increase their score. Creating sprite sheets and working out the collision detection made this a really fun little project in my downtime.\r\n\r\n![Gameplay demo](../../assets/projectImages/santa-game.gif)\r\n\r\nWe're hoping to reskin for the upcoming year and add a leaderboard. In the meantime, I'd [love to hear](mailto:hello@joannahosking.com) your highscore!","src/content/projects/santa.md",[47,48,49],"../../assets/projectImages/christmas-card.png","../../assets/projectImages/santa-game.gif","game","59bd6119acdfa628",{html:52,metadata:53},"<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://christmas.cooperfields.co.uk/\">Play now →</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every year, we put out a Christmas card for our clients at work. It’s usually a message wishing them a happy holiday season, but for 2023 we decided to do something a bit more creative.</p>\n<p><img __ASTRO_IMAGE_=\"{&#x22;src&#x22;:&#x22;../../assets/projectImages/christmas-card.png&#x22;,&#x22;alt&#x22;:&#x22;Christmas Card&#x22;,&#x22;index&#x22;:0}\">\r\n<em>Animated Christmas card cover</em></p>\n<p>Santa Dash is a simple pixel game built in canvas where the user collects coins and gifts to increase their score. Creating sprite sheets and working out the collision detection made this a really fun little project in my downtime.</p>\n<p><img __ASTRO_IMAGE_=\"{&#x22;src&#x22;:&#x22;../../assets/projectImages/santa-game.gif&#x22;,&#x22;alt&#x22;:&#x22;Gameplay demo&#x22;,&#x22;index&#x22;:0}\"></p>\n<p>We’re hoping to reskin for the upcoming year and add a leaderboard. In the meantime, I’d <a href=\"mailto:hello@joannahosking.com\">love to hear</a> your highscore!</p>",{headings:54,localImagePaths:55,remoteImagePaths:56,frontmatter:57,imagePaths:58},[],[47,48],[],{title:39,shortname:40,description:41,icon:49,sortOrder:43},[47,48],"santa.md","everything",{id:60,data:62,body:67,filePath:68,assetImports:69,digest:72,rendered:73,legacyId:81},{title:63,shortname:63,description:64,icon:65,sortOrder:66},"Everything Construction","ecommerce website","__ASTRO_IMAGE_shop",4,"- [Visit site &rarr;](https://cooperfields.co.uk)\r\n\r\n![Cooperfields website](../../assets/projectImages/cooperfields.png)\r\n\r\nI'm currently working as a developer at [Cooperfields](https://cooperfields.co.uk/). You can find [case studies](https://cooperfields.co.uk/case-studies/) of client work there or [get in touch](https://cooperfields.co.uk/contact/) about a potential project.","src/content/projects/everything.md",[70,71],"../../assets/projectImages/cooperfields.png","shop","0e680216b52b228f",{html:74,metadata:75},"<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://cooperfields.co.uk\">Visit site →</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><img __ASTRO_IMAGE_=\"{&#x22;src&#x22;:&#x22;../../assets/projectImages/cooperfields.png&#x22;,&#x22;alt&#x22;:&#x22;Cooperfields website&#x22;,&#x22;index&#x22;:0}\"></p>\n<p>I’m currently working as a developer at <a href=\"https://cooperfields.co.uk/\">Cooperfields</a>. You can find <a href=\"https://cooperfields.co.uk/case-studies/\">case studies</a> of client work there or <a href=\"https://cooperfields.co.uk/contact/\">get in touch</a> about a potential project.</p>",{headings:76,localImagePaths:77,remoteImagePaths:78,frontmatter:79,imagePaths:80},[],[70],[],{title:63,shortname:63,description:64,icon:71,sortOrder:66},[70],"everything.md","harris",{id:82,data:84,body:89,filePath:90,assetImports:91,digest:95,rendered:96,legacyId:104},{title:85,shortname:85,description:86,icon:87,sortOrder:88},"Harris Group","premium brochure site","__ASTRO_IMAGE_web",3,"- [Visit site &rarr;](https://harrispipework.co.uk/)\r\n\r\nHarris Pipework isn't one company but rather several distinct businesses under one group, and the site needed to communicate that \"group\" identity and portray them with the level of establishedness and trustworthiness they command across their industries, without feeling like a soulless holding-company page nobody actually wants to read.\r\n\r\n![Screenshot](../../assets/projectImages/harris-pipework-hero.png)\r\n\r\nThe approach was to lean into scale visually: large-format imagery, generous negative space, and scroll-driven animation built with *GSAP* to give the site an editorial, premium feel rather than a typical trade-industry template look. I worked closely with our designer to turn her vision into an actual animation plan, working through what was technically achievable, what would hold up for SEO given how much of the page relies on scroll-triggered reveals, and where we'd need to compromise between the two. We also collaborated on the UX side of a trickier problem: how do you present \"a group of companies\" in a way that feels coherent rather than like five separate mini-sites bolted together.\r\n\r\n![Scroll animation](../../assets/projectImages/harris-pipework-scroll.gif)\r\n\r\nLarge hero images and heavy animation are an easy way to tank performance if you're not careful, so I leaned on a few different fronts to keep it in check: debouncing scroll listeners where GSAP triggers didn't need to fire on every pixel of movement, serving WebP images with proper compression, and lazy loading below-the-fold imagery. Video was the bigger challenge, with several sections using large-scale background video, so alongside optimising and providing fallback imagery on the front end, I worked with the videography team directly on export settings, making sure their edits were coming through at file sizes and formats that wouldn't tank load times before they even reached the browser.","src/content/projects/harris.md",[92,93,94],"../../assets/projectImages/harris-pipework-hero.png","../../assets/projectImages/harris-pipework-scroll.gif","web","4ebd11d04eb7d26e",{html:97,metadata:98},"<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://harrispipework.co.uk/\">Visit site →</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Harris Pipework isn’t one company but rather several distinct businesses under one group, and the site needed to communicate that “group” identity and portray them with the level of establishedness and trustworthiness they command across their industries, without feeling like a soulless holding-company page nobody actually wants to read.</p>\n<p><img __ASTRO_IMAGE_=\"{&#x22;src&#x22;:&#x22;../../assets/projectImages/harris-pipework-hero.png&#x22;,&#x22;alt&#x22;:&#x22;Screenshot&#x22;,&#x22;index&#x22;:0}\"></p>\n<p>The approach was to lean into scale visually: large-format imagery, generous negative space, and scroll-driven animation built with <em>GSAP</em> to give the site an editorial, premium feel rather than a typical trade-industry template look. I worked closely with our designer to turn her vision into an actual animation plan, working through what was technically achievable, what would hold up for SEO given how much of the page relies on scroll-triggered reveals, and where we’d need to compromise between the two. We also collaborated on the UX side of a trickier problem: how do you present “a group of companies” in a way that feels coherent rather than like five separate mini-sites bolted together.</p>\n<p><img __ASTRO_IMAGE_=\"{&#x22;src&#x22;:&#x22;../../assets/projectImages/harris-pipework-scroll.gif&#x22;,&#x22;alt&#x22;:&#x22;Scroll animation&#x22;,&#x22;index&#x22;:0}\"></p>\n<p>Large hero images and heavy animation are an easy way to tank performance if you’re not careful, so I leaned on a few different fronts to keep it in check: debouncing scroll listeners where GSAP triggers didn’t need to fire on every pixel of movement, serving WebP images with proper compression, and lazy loading below-the-fold imagery. Video was the bigger challenge, with several sections using large-scale background video, so alongside optimising and providing fallback imagery on the front end, I worked with the videography team directly on export settings, making sure their edits were coming through at file sizes and formats that wouldn’t tank load times before they even reached the browser.</p>",{headings:99,localImagePaths:100,remoteImagePaths:101,frontmatter:102,imagePaths:103},[],[92,93],[],{title:85,shortname:85,description:86,icon:94,sortOrder:88},[92,93],"harris.md","steam",{id:105,data:107,body:111,filePath:112,assetImports:113,digest:117,rendered:118,legacyId:126},{title:108,shortname:108,description:109,icon:42,sortOrder:110},"Steam dashboard","tracking for your Steam library",1,"- [View on Github &rarr;](https://github.com/joannahosking/steam)\r\n- [Visit demo &rarr;](https://steam.joannahosking.com/)\r\n\r\nI wanted to get more comfortable with React and TypeScript patterns that don't really come up in my client work, things like handling multiple independent data sources loading at different speeds and dealing gracefully with third-party APIs that don't always cooperate. A Steam dashboard felt like a good excuse: pull your owned games, show genre breakdowns and backlog stats, and suggest what to play next from the pile you've never touched.\r\n\r\n![Search page](../../assets/projectImages/steam-search.png)\r\n\r\nSteam's own API gives you a library with playtime, but no genre data at all, so genres have to come from somewhere else. I started with *RAWG*, a free games database API, but partway through building this it went down for the better part of two days with no real explanation. That's not something I wanted the whole genre chart depending on, so I switched to Steam's own (undocumented, but stable) store endpoint as the primary source, keyed directly by app ID rather than fuzzy name matching, and kept RAWG around as a fallback for whatever Steam's endpoint can't answer.\r\n\r\n![Dashboard overview](../../assets/projectImages/steam-dashboard.png)\r\n\r\nSteam's store endpoint is informally rate-limited to roughly 200 requests per 5 minutes, which sounds like plenty until you test against a library with a couple thousand games in it. Enriching everything would blow past that instantly. So enrichment is capped: the most-played games, plus a sample of unplayed ones so the \"play next\" suggestions have something to work with, run through a small concurrency limiter I wrote so requests go out a few at a time instead of all at once or one by one. Results get cached by app ID too, so popular games (there's always a Stardew Valley or a Baldur's Gate 3 in there) only ever get looked up once, no matter how many different people's libraries include them.\r\n\r\n![Skeleton loading](../../assets/projectImages/steam-loading.gif)\r\n\r\nGenre data ended up being the slowest, least reliable part of the whole thing, so I didn't want it holding up everything else. Owned games, genre enrichment, and profile info all load as three separate queries. The stat cards, playtime chart, and full games table show up the moment your library loads, while the genre chart and suggestions sit in their own skeleton state and pop in once they're ready, instead of one spinner blocking the whole page on the slowest piece.","src/content/projects/steam.md",[114,115,116,49],"../../assets/projectImages/steam-search.png","../../assets/projectImages/steam-dashboard.png","../../assets/projectImages/steam-loading.gif","2260d2f2823892a9",{html:119,metadata:120},"<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/joannahosking/steam\">View on Github →</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://steam.joannahosking.com/\">Visit demo →</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>I wanted to get more comfortable with React and TypeScript patterns that don’t really come up in my client work, things like handling multiple independent data sources loading at different speeds and dealing gracefully with third-party APIs that don’t always cooperate. A Steam dashboard felt like a good excuse: pull your owned games, show genre breakdowns and backlog stats, and suggest what to play next from the pile you’ve never touched.</p>\n<p><img __ASTRO_IMAGE_=\"{&#x22;src&#x22;:&#x22;../../assets/projectImages/steam-search.png&#x22;,&#x22;alt&#x22;:&#x22;Search page&#x22;,&#x22;index&#x22;:0}\"></p>\n<p>Steam’s own API gives you a library with playtime, but no genre data at all, so genres have to come from somewhere else. I started with <em>RAWG</em>, a free games database API, but partway through building this it went down for the better part of two days with no real explanation. That’s not something I wanted the whole genre chart depending on, so I switched to Steam’s own (undocumented, but stable) store endpoint as the primary source, keyed directly by app ID rather than fuzzy name matching, and kept RAWG around as a fallback for whatever Steam’s endpoint can’t answer.</p>\n<p><img __ASTRO_IMAGE_=\"{&#x22;src&#x22;:&#x22;../../assets/projectImages/steam-dashboard.png&#x22;,&#x22;alt&#x22;:&#x22;Dashboard overview&#x22;,&#x22;index&#x22;:0}\"></p>\n<p>Steam’s store endpoint is informally rate-limited to roughly 200 requests per 5 minutes, which sounds like plenty until you test against a library with a couple thousand games in it. Enriching everything would blow past that instantly. So enrichment is capped: the most-played games, plus a sample of unplayed ones so the “play next” suggestions have something to work with, run through a small concurrency limiter I wrote so requests go out a few at a time instead of all at once or one by one. Results get cached by app ID too, so popular games (there’s always a Stardew Valley or a Baldur’s Gate 3 in there) only ever get looked up once, no matter how many different people’s libraries include them.</p>\n<p><img __ASTRO_IMAGE_=\"{&#x22;src&#x22;:&#x22;../../assets/projectImages/steam-loading.gif&#x22;,&#x22;alt&#x22;:&#x22;Skeleton loading&#x22;,&#x22;index&#x22;:0}\"></p>\n<p>Genre data ended up being the slowest, least reliable part of the whole thing, so I didn’t want it holding up everything else. Owned games, genre enrichment, and profile info all load as three separate queries. The stat cards, playtime chart, and full games table show up the moment your library loads, while the genre chart and suggestions sit in their own skeleton state and pop in once they’re ready, instead of one spinner blocking the whole page on the slowest piece.</p>",{headings:121,localImagePaths:122,remoteImagePaths:123,frontmatter:124,imagePaths:125},[],[114,115,116],[],{title:108,shortname:108,description:109,icon:49,sortOrder:110},[114,115,116],"steam.md"];

export { _astro_dataLayerContent as default };
